{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## /rebar-debug — Phase 1 RCA (root cause CONFIRMED at runtime)\n\n### Proven root cause\n\nThe materiality test is a **hash-equality check over the whole `file_impact` list**, so it is\nstructurally incapable of telling an ADDITION from a REMOVAL or a rewrite.\n\n`llm/plan_review/material_diff.py:110` `material_basis()` builds the basis with\n`\"file_impact\": ctx.state.get(\"file_impact\") or []`, and `_hash_component()` (:155) hashes that\nlist wholesale. Any mutation changes the digest; the digest is the only signal downstream\n(`llm/plan_review/attest.py:582` emits `\"verdict\": \"stale-material\"`).\n\nThe two gates therefore demand mutually exclusive things:\n\n- **Gate 1** — `_commands/close_precheck.py:302-310` computes `undeclared_paths(...)` and refuses\n  the close, instructing: *\"Declare them with `rebar set-file-impact <ticket> ...` **and retry**\"*.\n- **Gate 2** — following that instruction changes the `file_impact` hash, so the retry is refused\n  as `stale-material`.\n\n### Runtime proof (executed, not read)\n\nDriving the real functions from the installed package:\n\n```\nsigned  hash: c3ec16dd33b8418d\nwidened hash: c251cc50b5cd4233   CHANGED? True     <- purely ADDITIVE (+1 generated path)\nremoved hash: 9708be1d47b72913   CHANGED? True     <- REMOVAL\n```\n\nAn additive widening and a removal are **equally \"different\"**. There is no residue in the\ncomparison from which additivity could be recovered — confirming the cause is the hash-wholesale\ndesign, not a missing branch somewhere downstream.\n\nThe same run confirms the fix pattern already exists in this very module:\n\n```\ncheckbox tick normalized equal? True\n```\n\n`normalize_checkbox_state()` (`material_diff.py:66`) exists precisely so ticking an AC checkbox\ndoes not stale an attestation. So \"normalize away a change that must not count as material\" is an\nestablished, shipped concept here — the fix extends a precedent rather than inventing one.\n\n### Legitimacy: `defect` (not intended behavior)\n\nCited authority — `_commands/close_precheck.py:307-309`. The gate's own remediation text promises\n`\"and retry\"`. A remediation instruction that the system then rejects is a broken contract on its\nface; this is not a matter of the reporter's expectation.\n\n### The constraint any fix must respect\n\n`docs/plan-review-gate.md:1062` states the purpose of `stale-material` verbatim:\n\n> `stale-material` — you attested and then edited the plan — still blocks, **so the gate is not\n> bypassable by editing after signing.**\n\nThis **rules out** the broadest candidate the ticket floats (\"a purely ADDITIVE `file_impact`\ncorrection is a candidate for not staling the attestation\"). Blanket-additive would create exactly\nthe bypass the gate exists to prevent: after signing, an author could add any path — say\n`src/rebar/_commands/txn.py` — and close against work no reviewer ever saw. Additive is not\nsynonymous with harmless.\n\nWhat is genuinely safe is narrower: the paths in Gate 1's refusal are **computed by the gate from\nthe commit** (`commit_impact.undeclared_paths`), not chosen by the author. A widening whose\nadditions are a subset of what the gate itself just demanded introduces no author discretion, so\nit cannot be a bypass vector. That distinction — gate-directed vs author-chosen — is the seam the\nfix should cut along, and it is strictly narrower than \"additive\".\n\n### Cross-link to the P1\n\n`close_precheck.py:328` runs `subprocess.run([\"git\", \"-C\", ..., \"log\", \"--format=...\"])` over the\nrepository's entire history with **no `timeout=`** — one of the 61 unguarded subprocess sites found\nwhile investigating `unquieting-godsent-mammal` (`b405-d5ec-30b3-4538`), and it sits on the\n`transition -> closed` path, which is one of the two operations reported hanging there. Recorded as\nan observation; not claimed as that bug's cause, which is still under investigation.\n\n### Not yet done\n\nDefect attribution (`caused_by`) is still outstanding and will be established before any close, per\nprotocol. Fix design is a security-gate semantics decision and is being put to the operator rather\nthan chosen unilaterally."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787251365364865001,"uuid":"fc26b492-60f0-4b16-96fa-6728856bbcfa"}